<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Einstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Einstein was a German-born physicist, born in Ulm, whose theories of special and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">general relativity</a> transformed physics. In 1905, while working at the Swiss patent office in Bern, he published papers on relativity, light quanta and Brownian motion, and he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics for 1921. Of Jewish origin, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1933 and settled in Princeton in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-einstein.jpg" alt="Albert Einstein" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren Jack Turner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Einstein_Head_cleaned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Albert Einstein</b> was born in Ulm in 1879, though his family soon moved to Munich.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Federal Polytechnic School in Zurich, founded in 1855 and now ETH Zurich, became one of the leading technical universities, and in 1905 <b>Albert Einstein</b>, then a clerk at the patent office in Bern, published his papers on relativity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bertrand Russell argued that it weakens the individual’s capacity to judge his country’s foreign policy; <b>Albert Einstein</b> called it “an infantile disease … the measles of mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>The economist Alexander Sachs delivered to Franklin Roosevelt a letter signed by <b>Albert Einstein</b>, warning that Germany might build a uranium bomb.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The minster spire was completed in 1890, and <b>Albert Einstein</b> was born in Ulm in 1879.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-ulm">Free Imperial City of Ulm</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ulm, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Princeton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.4,9.99&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Ulm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4782 471 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert Einstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5232 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5232 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.4,9.99&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
